## Background

COVID-19 had been spreading around the world since December 2019, when the first case was reported in Wuhan, China. Data scientists were analyzing the trends and building prediction models to help policymakers decide how to slow the spread. Regression is one of several ways to predict where an outbreak is heading.

## Data and scope

- Regions: the entire world, Iran, Canada, and Canada's provinces.
- Time window: 22 January to 7 April 2020.
- Data from the CSSE at Johns Hopkins University.

## Part 1: visualization and analysis

For each region, visualizations and explanations of:

- cumulative infected, recovered, and dead cases, both in absolute numbers and per population, and
- the mortality rate and recovery rate over time,

to find what the overall trends had to say.

## Part 2: prediction

Three regression models, Polynomial Regression, Bayesian Ridge Regression, and Support Vector Regression, each modelled the total infected cases for the world, Iran, and Canada separately. The project predicts the future situation in each area and compares the three models, to show how their strengths differ from one dataset to another.
